A||||2||RICK||Aftermarket ignition
Z||000000||RICK||04-26-99||05:03 PM||||I'm looking for sugustions on putting an aftermarket ignition system on the 2.8.<br>The motor is being modified with higher compresion pistons (forged), crane cam, roller rockers, double spring valve springs, port & polish, headers, high volume fuel pump, NOS....<br>The ignition system is the last thing I need to buy before it all get's put together.  Thanks for any help
Z||000001||Punkndrublic||04-26-99||07:24 PM||||I was reading a few peoples pages. One person tried an msd ignition and it fried in two months. Another used some aftermarket one from pontiac and it work real good. The jacob electronics one was given a good review too. I think as long as it withstands the heat and is multiple spark and allows you to add in more features, like timing retards and stuff then it should be good. Personaly, I like what I ve read and heard bout the Jacobs Electronics one, so I going for that unless of course someone has a reason why I shouldn't
Z||000002||theogre||04-26-99||07:33 PM||||we'll help you here all we can, but you should also talk directly to someone who does exactly that kind of electrical work.<p>The stock distributor should not be a problem, but you may need to do things like find a better cap and rotor when you up the ignition power. I personally like MSW's helical over silicon/carbon plug wire. no radio noise, hot spark. nice price. off the shelf availability. (my 87 has DIS coils. you Must use good wires on this system or you Will have trouble.)<p>you should also check out Accel, Moroso, MSW and other high energy component maker web sites. There are +- issues to any choice you make. The best choice for you specific setup is something we can only guess at here.<p>BTW, if you are using NOS I strongly recomend you consult the NOS system maker before selecting pistons. The best NOS setup doesn't always need higher compression. NOS systems will work on anything, but the best streetable NOS systems are often on close to stock compression engines.
